I really enjoyed Elon Musk’s interview with Andrew Ross Sorkin. While I always appreciate Elon’s candor, I don’t understand his long-term goal of interstellar travel. $SPCX should focus on achievable and economically viable goals like orbital space stations or outposts on the Moon.

The distance between stars is too vast, and the speeds at which we can realistically travel, especially given the fuel constraints of chemical rockets, are much too slow to make interstellar travel for biological humans feasible.

Plus, it’s not just humans who are constrained by the laws of physics, geometry, spacetime, and the reality of the universe. It’s highly unlikely that there are any other spacefaring civilizations, no matter how advanced their propulsion systems, that are capable of interstellar travel either.

Like @elonmusk, I’m also a Star Trek fan. It would be great if there really were a way to travel faster than light at warp speed through subspace, circumventing the insurmountable obstacles of speed, fuel, mass, distance, the interstellar medium, and time dilation that combine to make interstellar travel through actual spacetime realistically impossible.

When it comes to interstellar travel or a federation of planets, science fiction really is fiction. Elon should focus his genius on the aspects of science fiction that are scientifically possible.

@0xBklyn I've got no issue with dreaming about interstellar travel and alien encounters. I just understand that there is no point planning for it.

@CaptBelowMe Well I know enough about this to realize Elon has no chance at pulling it off.

@loehndorfly I'm sure he does. He is much smarter than I am. That's why I don't understand why he keeps talking about it.

@MichaelM767588 @elonmusk I think you would reconsider once you understood what you would be in for. Space travel is glamorized in the movies. I'd be up for a short trip to the moon. But going to Mars should not be on anyone's bucket list.

@PastorPoundTwn @elonmusk I'm sure he's thought of them all. He is much smarter than I am. That's why I don't understand his obsession with building that city in the first place.

@orcabayio @elonmusk The comparison is not even close to being relevant.  In fact, the economic payoff was worth the cost, which is why the trip was funded.

@TheKingofBattle @elonmusk He was not talking about a virtual city for uploaded consciousness. He spoke about an actual physical city with biological humans living there.

@MrWilliamBonney @elonmusk There is nothing there. It's a frigid desert with a toxic atmosphere.

@nyeric @elonmusk I wrote every word. And yes, I would like an answer.

@MichaelM767588 @elonmusk Getting to Mars is not impossible, though there is a good chance you would not survive the trip. But it's not worth it. A city on Mars is not viable. What does make sense is sending AI robots there. We will learn a lot more that way, at a much lower cost, without risking humans.

@White_Lykos @elonmusk Not if they all die along the way or in a crash landing. Apart from the health issues, this is a very risky mission. I'm not even sure SpaceX can solve the engineering problems to pull off a successful manned mission to Mars.

@310Ventures @elonmusk Just to check the spelling.

@CDisco5 @elonmusk More like die on Mars, or on the way to Mars.

@310Ventures @elonmusk Well it's more likely that some humans can survive an extinction event on earth, or even on the moon, then on Mars. If we are going to build an underground city, it's much easier to build one here on earth, that is far more accessible if it's ever needed.

@Jay_Star_21 @elonmusk Yes, trying to colonizing Mars would be a complete waste of resources and human life. It's certainly not something SpaceX should finance.

@realalexwiggin @elonmusk It goes without saying that would help.

@LinusCaldwell5 @elonmusk Expanding into the galaxy is even more ridiculous that trying to colonize Mars. Interstellar travel is basically banned by the laws of geometry, physics, and the reality of spacetime itself.

